          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Linear logic
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substructural+logic"} substructural logic
            in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction rule
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            are omitted, or at least have their applicability restricted.
          %h3#AbsenceOfWeakeningAndContraction Absence of weakening and contraction
          %p
            Notice that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening
            are the two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rule"} structural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            which exhibit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extensions"} context extensions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \,\mapsto\, \Gamma, P
            as admitting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+map"} diagonal
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maps"} maps
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpretation
            as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+products"} cartesian products
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \times P
            (cf.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "structural+rule#Jacobs94"} Jacobs 1994
          %div{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/WeakeningContractionRules-230330d.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %p
            Therefore, when these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            are dropped then the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            of contexts may still have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpretation
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            (such as known from the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            (such as known from the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+product+space"} product of topological spaces
          %p
            Accordingly, if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
            still follows the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rule"} inference rule
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+types"} function types
            but now with respect to a non-cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi P
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi p
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi T
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo ↦
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi P
                      %mo ⊸
                      %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{
                  \\Gamma \otimes P
                  \\;\;\vdash\;\;
                  t_p \,\colon\, T
                  }
                  {
                  \\Gamma
                  \\;\;\vdash\;\;
                  (p \mapsto t_p)
                  \\,\colon\,
                  P \multimap T
                  }
          %p
            etc., then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \multimap T
            may be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpreted
            as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            in a non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            — such as the category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi P
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash f \colon P \multimap T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpret
            as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functions"} linear functions
            — whence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+connective"} logical connective
            “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊸
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \multimap
            ” is also called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+implication"} linear implication
          %p
            Which exact flavour of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            provides
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for linear logic depends on exactly which further
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            are considered (see also at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bunched+logic"} bunched logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Girard1987"} Girard (1987)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of/has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+categories"} star-autonomous categories
            (
            %a{:href => "#Seely"}> Seely 89, prop. 1.5
            ). But more generally
            %em linear logic
            came to refer to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of any possibly-non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            (then usually called
            %em multiplicative intuitionistic linear logic
            –
            = succeed ")" do
              %em MILL
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polycategory"} polycategory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Szabo78"} Szabo 78
            see the
            %a{:href => "linear%20type%20theory#HistoryCategoricalSemantics"} history section
            and see also
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#dePaiva89"} de Paiva 89
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Blute91"} Blute 91
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BentonBiermanPaivaHyland92"} Benton–Bierman–de Paiva–Hyland 92
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HylandPaiva93"} Hyland–de Paiva 93
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bierman95"} Bierman 95
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Barber97"} Barber 97
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schalk04"} Schalk 04
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Mellies09"} Melliès 09
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+nets"} proof nets
            (or the associated
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kelly%E2%80%93Mac+Lane+graphs"} Kelly–Mac Lane graphs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            for monoidal categories.
          %p
            Indeed, these more general senses of linear logic still faithfully follow the original motivation for the term “linear” as connoting “resource availability” explained
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ResourceAvailability"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            means the absence of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
            map and hence the impossibility of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            to depend on more than a single (linear) copy of their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
          %p
            In addition to these usual “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denotational+semantics"}> denotational
            ” categorical semantics, linear logic also has an “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operational+semantics"}> operational
            ” categorical semantics, called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry+of+Interaction"} Geometry of Interaction
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/traced+monoidal+categories"} traced monoidal categories
          %p
            Although linear logic is traditionally presented in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+spaces"} coherence spaces
          %h3#AsAQuantumLogic As a quantum logic
          %p
            With the archetypical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of linear logic in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite dimensional vector spaces
            in mind, which is the habitat of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} there
            for more), one may naturally understand linear logic as a form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Pratt92"}> Pratt (1992)
            \&amp;rbrack; and in its natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} enhancement
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            also as a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit"} quantum circuit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+language"} language
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#AbramskyDuncan06"}> Abramsky &amp; Duncan (2006)
            ;
            = succeed "&rbrack;." do
              %a{:href => "#Duncan06"} Duncan (2006)
          %blockquote
            %p#DalLagoFaggianQuote
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#DalLagoFaggian12"}> Dal Lago &amp; Faggian (2012)
              \&amp;rbrack;: “It’s more and more clear that strong relationships exist between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no-cloning+theorem"} impossibility of cloning and erasing
              qubits.”
            %p
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#Staton15"}> Staton (2015), p. 1
              \&amp;rbrack;: “A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+language"} quantum programming language
              captures the ideas of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
              in a
              = succeed ".\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
          %p Indeed:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the absence of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction rule
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#AbsenceOfWeakeningAndContraction"} above
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                is the
                %em duplication
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data"} data
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no-cloning+theorem"} no-cloning theorem
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %li
              %p
                the absence of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#AbsenceOfWeakeningAndContraction"} above
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                is the
                %em erasure
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data"} data
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/no-deleting+theorem"} no-deleting theorem
            %li
              %p
                the non-cartesian
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                is given by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+products"} tensor products
                (such as
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+Hilbert+spaces"} of Hilbert spaces
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} entangled
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "#Baez04"}> Baez (2004)
                \&amp;rbrack;.
          %p
            Historically, linear logic was not explicitly motivated as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            this way, but as a logic keeping track of “
            %em> resource availability
            ” (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#ResourceAvailability"} below
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction rule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variable"} variable
            could, a priori, be “only used once” and in fact (due to the absence of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            (for example, the
            = succeed "-protocol" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+teleportation"} quantum teleportation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell+states"} Bell states
            as a resource that allow for and are consumed by the intended “teleportation” operation), see the references
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "quantum+information#ReferencesQuantumResources"} there
          %h3#ResourceAvailability As a logic of resource availability
          %p
            Unlike many traditional formal systems of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth"} truth
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#Girard1987"} Girard (1987)
            %em availability
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %em resources
          %p
            A proposition, if it is true, remains true no matter how we use that fact in proving other propositions. By contrast, in using a resource
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to make available a resource
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            itself may be consumed or otherwise modified. Linear logic deals with this by restricting our ability to duplicate or discard resources freely.
          %p For example:
          %blockquote
            %p Let’s please add a better example!
          %p We have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext have cake
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mtext eat cake
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{have cake} \vdash \text{eat cake}
          %p from which we can prove
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext have cake
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext have cake
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mtext have cake
                  %mo ∧
                  %mtext eat cake
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\text{have cake},\; \text{have cake} \vdash \text{have cake}
                  \\wedge \text{eat cake}
          %p
            which by left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction
            (duplication of inputs) in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            yields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext have cake
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mtext have cake
                  %mo ∧
                  %mtext eat cake
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{have cake} \vdash \text{have cake} \wedge \text{eat cake}
          %p
            Linear logic would disallow the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction
            step and treat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext have cake
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext have cake
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{have cake},\; \text{have cake} \vdash A
            as explicitly meaning that
            %em two
            slices of cake yield
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h3#game_semantics Game semantics
          %p Linear logic can also be interpreted using a semantics of “games” or “interactions”. Under this interpretation, each proposition in a sequent represents a game being played or a transaction protocol being executed. An assertion of, for instance,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P, Q \vdash R
          %p
            means roughly that if I am playing three simultaneous games of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            and the right player in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            For example,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \vdash P
            can be won by using the copycat strategy where one makes the two games identical except with left and right players reversed.
          %h3#as_a_relevance_logic As a relevance logic
          %p
            Linear logic is closely related to notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relevance+logic"} relevance logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
            = succeed "," do
              %em exactly once
            %em at least
            once.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Linear logic is usually given in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            (although as remarked above, to be thought of more as resources to be acquired than as statements to be proved) which we construct through
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lists"} lists
            of propositions is a
            %strong sequent
            (written as usual with ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash
            ’ between the lists), some of which are
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong valid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+calculus"} propositional calculus
            of linear logic also requires a set of
            %strong propositional variables
            from which to start; this is usually identified with the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            (so the variables are
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LL(V)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is any initial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of propositional variables.
          %p Here we define the set of propositions:
          %ul
            %li
              %p Every propositional variable is a proposition.
            %li
              %p
                For each proposition
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^\perp
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                For each proposition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and proposition
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mi &amp;
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \&amp; B
                    (read ‘with’), the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+conjunction"} additive conjunction
                    of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                    and
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi B
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊕
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \oplus B
                    (read ‘plus’), the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+disjunction"} additive disjunction
                    of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                    and
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi B
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
                    (read ‘times’), the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
                    of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                    and
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi B
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⅋
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \parr B
                    (read ‘par’), the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+disjunction"} multiplicative disjunction
                    of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                    and
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                    (sometimes written
                    = succeed ")." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                            %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                            %mi B
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \mid B
            %li
              %p There are also four constants to go with the four binary operations above:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊤
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
                    (read ‘top’), the
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %strong
                        additive
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth"} truth
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{0}
                    (read ‘zero’), the
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %strong
                        additive
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/falsity"} falsity
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
                    (read ‘one’), the
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %strong multiplicative truth
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊥
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
                    (read ‘bottom’), the
                    = succeed "." do
                      %strong multiplicative falsity
            %li
              %p
                For each proposition
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo !
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !{A}
                    (read ‘
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/of+course"}> of course
                    ’), the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+conjunction"} exponential conjunction
                    of
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %li
                  %p
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ?
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ?{A}
                    (read ‘
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/why+not"}> why not
                    ’), the
                    %strong
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+disjunction"} exponential disjunction
                    of
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            The terms “exponential”, “multiplicative”, and “additive” come from the fact that “exponentiation converts addition to multiplication”: we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mi &amp;
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≡
                  %mo !
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo !
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !{(A \&amp; B)}\equiv !{A} \otimes !{B}
            and so on (see below).
          %p
            However, the connectives and constants can also be grouped in different ways. For instance, the multiplicative conjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            and additive disjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            are both
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+types"} positive types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi &amp;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \&amp;
            and multiplicative disjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⅋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+types"} negative types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            and the additive falsity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{0}
            are positive, while the additive truth
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            and multiplicative falsity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            are negative. This grouping has the advantage that the similarity of symbols matches the adjective used (because the symbols were chosen with this grouping in mind).
          %p
            But conversely, the natural grouping by multiplicative/additive, or equivalently by de Morgan dual pairs, has led many authors to alter Girard’s notation, in particular reverting to the category-theoretic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            for the additives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi &amp;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \&amp;
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊕
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            or (confusingly)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            for Girard’s
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⅋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relevance+logic"} relevance logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi &amp;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \&amp;
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊕
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∨
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes B
            is called
            %strong fusion
            and denoted
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\circ B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⅋
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\parr B
            is called
            %strong fission
            and denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A+B
            (or sometimes, confusingly,
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\oplus B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            may also be used for the
            %em additive
            falsity, here denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{0}
            %strong extensional
            and the multiplicatives
            = succeed "." do
              %strong intensional
          %p
            Sometimes one does not define the operation of negation, defining only
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^\perp
            for a propositional variable
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            We now define the valid sequents, where we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B, C \vdash D, E, F
            to state the validity of the sequent consisting of the list
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,B,C)
            and the list
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (D,E,F)
            and use Greek letters for sublists:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    The
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exchange+rule"} exchange rule
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation"} permutation
                    of it (created by permuting its left and right sides independently) is valid.
                %li
                  %p
                    The restricted
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi Γ
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo ⊢
                            %mi Θ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, \Delta \vdash \Theta
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi Γ
                            %mo ,
                            %mo !
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo ⊢
                            %mi Θ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, !{A}, \Delta \vdash \Theta
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi Γ
                            %mo ⊢
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Θ
